{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgtJak8mJSJm6L6r7CvD7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The ablation shows the four induction heads matter but are not the whole story. Removing them lowers loop-token probability only from 0.997 to 0.95 (text A) and 0.92 (text B). It costs pre-loop text almost as much (log-prob −0.11 and −0.15 per token), and the start of each repeated line remains near-certain. So the loop is overdetermined: other heads and/or DeltaNet layers carry the copy as well, and cutting induction heads is a blunt trade-off against normal in-context copying. A more targeted lever could be \"repetition neurons\" — MLP neurons, reported in other LMs, whose activation builds up during repetition and which promote continuing it. I have every MLP activation for both texts from pass 2. I'll look for neurons that switch on much more strongly inside the loop than in the pre-loop reasoning, consistently across both texts, and check whether their activation rises with the number of completed cycles. That rise would be the self-reinforcement signature.\n\n```python\n# Hypothesis: specific MLP neurons are consistently elevated inside loops (vs pre-loop) in both texts -> \"repetition neurons\".\nstats = []\nfor layer in range(24):\n    M = np.load(D5 + f'mlp_act_{layer}.npy', mmap_mode='r')\n    zs = []\n    for b in range(2):\n        L = lens5[b]; on = loop_start_tok[b]\n        X = np.asarray(M[b, :L], dtype=np.float32)\n        pre = X[150:on]; lp = X[on+64:L]\n        sd = X[150:L].std(0) + 1e-4\n        zs.append(((lp.mean(0) - pre.mean(0)) / sd, lp.mean(0), pre.mean(0)))\n    zmin = np.minimum(zs[0][0], zs[1][0])        # consistently elevated in loop in both texts\n    zmax = np.maximum(zs[0][0], zs[1][0])        # consistently reduced\n    for n in np.argsort(-zmin)[:3]:\n        stats.append(dict(layer=layer, neuron=int(n), kind='up', z0=zs[0][0][n], z1=zs[1][0][n], loop0=zs[0][1][n], pre0=zs[0][2][n], loop1=zs[1][1][n], pre1=zs[1][2][n]))\n    for n in np.argsort(zmax)[:2]:\n        stats.append(dict(layer=layer, neuron=int(n), kind='down', z0=zs[0][0][n], z1=zs[1][0][n], loop0=zs[0][1][n], pre0=zs[0][2][n], loop1=zs[1][1][n], pre1=zs[1][2][n]))\nS = pd.DataFrame(stats)\nS['zc'] = np.where(S.kind=='up', np.minimum(S.z0, S.z1), -np.maximum(S.z0, S.z1))\nprint(S.sort_values('zc', ascending=False).head(20).round(3).to_string())\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1645,"cache_read_input_tokens":97707,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1645},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37ae3ac5-393a-4da3-8caf-f8ba0ea8f929","uuid":"7f70063b-306b-4708-b569-5942814b0bd3","timestamp":"2026-11-10T00:31:33.420Z","request_id":"req_011CgtJajXYMAXrUzDrZdyNT"}
